    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d22">

                <pb facs="0325" n="11" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg11"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.0012/575</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Guthrie</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-11-05T16:00:00+09:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 5, 1914, 4 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 a.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your telegram of October 16, 6 p.m. I have again spoken to the Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs about peace treaty but have failed to secure any expression from
                    him. From the trend of his remarks I infer that he is not ready to adopt the
                    policy of such treaties. He is also inclined to the belief that the United
                    States and Japan have conflicting views in regard to China. There is also always
                    present strong dissatisfaction about California legislation intensified by fear
                    that hostile legislation will be passed. We can hardly hope for a favorable
                    response at least until that fear is removed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Guthrie</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
